# Tessera stdlib: preemptive threads on an AArch64 board without an operating system, in round robin
#
# Without an operating system nothing hands a program threads, so the program brings the manager an OS would have
# had: ThreadManagerByRoundRobin manages the threads of one core. It runs each ready thread for a slice of time, and
# when the slice is over the timer's interrupt takes the core from it (preemption) and gives it to the next ready one,
# in the order they became ready. A thread may give the core up earlier: `yield_now`, `sleep(span)`, or
# `wait_on(at, expected)` until another thread's `wake_one(at)` / `wake_all(at)` (the free routines of Wait.te go to
# the running manager's). When no thread is ready the core waits for the next interrupt (`wfi`) on the idle thread.
#
# The threads' memory is a region the program names (a global array, say): the manager cuts it into slots of
# `stack_size` bytes, one per thread, each holding the thread's record at its bottom, its thread-local block above it,
# a check word above that, and the thread's stack, which a StackManager manages, above that. A slot comes back when its thread's routine returns, for
# the next `launch`. While a thread runs, RUNNING_STACK (StackManager_freestanding.te) is its stack, so the frame check
# at the start of each routine measures from the thread's own stack bottom: a stack that runs out stops the program with
# `StackOverflowError` before it leaves its slot. The check word catches what wrote below the stack without a frame
# check, when the thread next stops. The first slot is the idle thread's.
#
# The timer is the core's virtual timer (CpuTimer.te) on the line an InterruptManagerByGic holds. `run()`
# turns it on: the flow that calls it joins the threads, waits until every thread launched has returned, and turns it
# off again. Each thread keeps its own failure mold and its own crash trace depth: the switch carries them
# (Switch.te's switch_fiber, Trace.te's trace_switch). Each thread has its own `@threadlocal` globals too: the switch
# points TPIDR_EL0 at the block in the thread's slot (the flow of `run` keeps the block prepare_core gave it), and a
# thread starts with every one at its starting value.
#
# A thread's state: READY (in the ready queue), RUNNING (on the core), WAITING (on a word), SLEEPING (until a counter
# value), and FREE for a slot no thread holds. Every change of state happens with the core's interrupts masked, and
# every switch too: the thread that resumes puts its own mask back (an interrupted one by returning from the
# interrupt, one that gave the core up by restoring what it saved, a new one by unmasking).
#
# A switch runs where the thread stops: inside the timer's interrupt handler, on the interrupted thread's stack, for a
# preemption, and inside yield_now, sleep, or wait_on otherwise. The CpuEventManager's IRQ entry saves the registers
# a routine may change and the interrupted place (ELR and SPSR) on that stack, and switch_stack saves the rest, so a
# thread preempted on one path may be resumed from any other.

module Standard/Unhosted

import Standard/Fiber

### The manager whose timer is running, for the timer's interrupt handler: null outside `run`.
@target(arch: "aarch64", os: "none")
secret global RUNNING_ROUND_ROBIN: &@ThreadManagerByRoundRobin

### The word written above a thread's record, below its stack: a stack that ran down into its record changes it first.
@target(arch: "aarch64", os: "none")
secret preset STACK_CHECK_WORD: U64 = 0x57AC4CA9A2E5AFE0

### Where a thread of a ThreadManagerByRoundRobin is.
@target(arch: "aarch64", os: "none")
choice RoundRobinState: U8
    FREE  # no thread holds the slot
    READY  # in the ready queue, waiting for the core
    RUNNING  # on the core
    WAITING  # waiting on a word until a wake
    SLEEPING  # waiting until the counter reaches its time

### One thread of a ThreadManagerByRoundRobin: its stack, its state, and its place in one of the manager's lists.
@target(arch: "aarch64", os: "none")
secret entity RoundRobinThread
    ### Its stack. Its `sp` is the thread's stack pointer while it isn't running.
    stack: StackManager
    state: RoundRobinState
    ### The next thread of the list it is in (the free slots, the ready queue, the sleepers, or the waiters).
    next: @RoundRobinThread
    ### The counter value it sleeps until, while SLEEPING.
    wake_at: U64
    ### The word it waits on, while WAITING.
    waits_on: Addr
    ### The routine the thread runs, and what it is given.
    entry: Callable[(Addr,), None]
    arg: Addr
    ### Its crash trace depth while it isn't running.
    trace_depth: U64
    ### Its number, counted from 1 in the order threads were launched, 0 for the idle thread and the flow of `run`.
    number: U64
    ### Its thread-local block, which TPIDR_EL0 points at while it runs.
    thread_locals: Addr

### Preemptive round robin threads on one core: each ready thread runs for a slice, then the next one does.
@target(arch: "aarch64", os: "none")
entity ThreadManagerByRoundRobin
    ### The memory the threads' slots are cut from.
    region: Addr
    ### The bytes of each slot: a thread's record, its check word, and its stack.
    slot_size: U64
    ### How many slots the region holds, the idle thread's first.
    slot_count: U64
    ### The slice of time a thread runs before the next ready one, in counter ticks.
    slice: U64
    ### The slots no thread holds.
    free: @RoundRobinThread
    ### The ready queue: the first one to run next, the last one most recently ready.
    ready_first: @RoundRobinThread
    ready_last: @RoundRobinThread
    ### The sleeping threads, the earliest to wake first.
    sleeping: @RoundRobinThread
    ### The threads waiting on a word.
    waiting: @RoundRobinThread
    ### The thread on the core.
    running: @RoundRobinThread
    ### The flow that called `run`, on the stack it had.
    boot: RoundRobinThread
    ### The idle thread, which waits for interrupts when no other thread is ready.
    idle: @RoundRobinThread
    ### The threads launched that haven't returned yet.
    live: U64
    ### Counts the threads that returned: what `run` waits on.
    finished: U32
    ### The number the next thread launched gets.
    next_number: U64
    ### How many times the timer's interrupt came while the core waited on the idle thread.
    idle_wakes: U64
    ### How many times the timer took the core from a running thread and gave it to another.
    preemptions: U64
    ### The interrupt lines the timer's line is on.
    interrupts: @InterruptManagerByGic
    ### The handler of the timer's line before `run`.
    timer_before: Addr

### A thread's record over `stack`, in no list, with no routine yet.
### :param stack: Its stack.
### :param state: Its state.
### :returns: The record.
@target(arch: "aarch64", os: "none")
secret common routine RoundRobinThread.construct(stack: StackManager, state: RoundRobinState) -> Me
    return Me(stack: steal stack, state, next: .hollow(), wake_at: 0, waits_on: .hollow(),
        entry: .hollow(), arg: .hollow(), trace_depth: 0, number: 0, thread_locals: .hollow())

### Gives the boot flow's and the idle thread's stack managers back (the memory under them is the program's).
@target(arch: "aarch64", os: "none")
secret routine @RoundRobinThread.destruct() -> None
    drop me.stack
    return

### Gives the boot flow's record back. The region's memory is the program's: it outlives the manager.
@target(arch: "aarch64", os: "none")
routine @ThreadManagerByRoundRobin.destruct() -> None
    drop me.boot
    return

### Makes a manager whose threads live in the `size` bytes at `region`, each in a slot of `stack_size` bytes, and run
### for `slice` at a time.
### :param region: The first byte of the memory for the threads, 16-byte aligned (a global array, say).
### :param size: The region's size.
### :param stack_size: The size of each thread's slot: its record, its check word, and its stack.
### :param slice: How long a thread runs before the next ready one does.
### :param interrupts: The interrupt lines, started already, the timer's line among them.
### :returns: The manager: the idle thread in the first slot, the other slots free for `launch`.
### :throws: Crashes with `UnacceptedValueError` when the region holds fewer than two slots or a slot is too small for a
###     thread's record and 1 KiB of stack.
@target(arch: "aarch64", os: "none")
@track_caller
common routine ThreadManagerByRoundRobin.construct(region: Addr, size: ByteSize,
    stack_size: ByteSize, slice: Duration, interrupts: @InterruptManagerByGic) -> Me
    slot_size: U64 = stack_size.bytes() - (stack_size.bytes() & 15)
    if slot_size < record_room() + 1024 or size.bytes() // slot_size < 2
        throw UnacceptedValueError(description: "The region holds fewer than two slots of stack_size, or stack_size has no room for a thread's record and 1 KiB of stack.")
    return Me(region, slot_size, slot_count: size.bytes() // slot_size,
        slice: cpu_timer_ticks(slice).max(1), free: .hollow(), ready_first: .hollow(),
        ready_last: .hollow(), sleeping: .hollow(), waiting: .hollow(), running: .hollow(),
        boot: RoundRobinThread.construct(stack: StackManager.construct(bottom: .hollow(), top: .hollow()), state: RoundRobinState.RUNNING),
        idle: .hollow(), live: 0, finished: 0, next_number: 1, idle_wakes: 0, preemptions: 0,
        interrupts, timer_before: .hollow())

### Starts a thread that runs `entry(arg)`, in a free slot: it is ready, and runs once `run` gives it the core. Its
### slot comes back when `entry` returns.
### :param entry: The thread's routine.
### :param arg: What it is given.
### :returns: False when no slot is free.
@target(arch: "aarch64", os: "none")
routine @ThreadManagerByRoundRobin.launch(entry: Callable[(Addr,), None], arg: Addr) -> Bool
    was: U64 = mask_interrupts()
    me.prepare_slots()
    thread: @RoundRobinThread = me.free.peek()
    if thread.is_hollow()
        restore_interrupts(was)
        return false
    me.free.poke(thread.next.peek())
    thread.entry.poke(entry)
    thread.arg.poke(arg)
    thread.number.poke(me.next_number.peek())
    me.next_number.peek().add(1).poke_into(me.next_number)
    thread.trace_depth.poke(0)
    thread.stack.sp.poke(initial_stack(stack: thread.stack.bottom.peek(),
        size: thread.stack.size().bytes(), entry: round_robin_thread_main.to[Callable]().addr()))
    me.live.peek().add(1).poke_into(me.live)
    me.make_ready(thread)
    restore_interrupts(was)
    return true

### Starts a thread for a caller that hands over the manager as an address: `(entry, arg, state)` with the
### manager's address as `state`.
### :param entry: The thread's routine.
### :param arg: What it is given.
### :param state: The ThreadManagerByRoundRobin.
### :returns: False when no slot is free.
@target(arch: "aarch64", os: "none")
routine launch_round_robin(entry: Callable[(Addr,), None], arg: Addr, state: Addr) -> Bool
    manager: @ThreadManagerByRoundRobin = state.to[@ThreadManagerByRoundRobin]()
    return manager.launch(entry, arg)

### Runs the threads launched: turns the timer's line and the timer on, joins them on the stack of the flow that calls
### it, and returns once every thread launched (before or while it runs) has returned. Threads launched later wait for
### the next `run`.
@target(arch: "aarch64", os: "none")
routine @ThreadManagerByRoundRobin.run() -> None
    was: U64 = mask_interrupts()
    me.prepare_slots()
    boot_stack: @StackManager = StackManager.current()
    me.boot.stack.bottom.poke(boot_stack.bottom.peek())
    me.boot.stack.top.poke(boot_stack.top.peek())
    me.boot.state.poke(RoundRobinState.RUNNING)
    me.boot.thread_locals.poke(thread_pointer())
    me.running.poke(me.boot)
    RUNNING_ROUND_ROBIN.poke(me)
    gic: @InterruptManagerByGic = me.interrupts.peek()
    before: Callable[(U32,), None] = gic.when_line_raised(line: CPU_TIMER_LINE,
        holder: round_robin_tick.to[Callable]())
    me.timer_before.poke(before.addr())
    gic.turn_on(CPU_TIMER_LINE)
    me.arm_timer()
    unmask_interrupts()
    loop
        seen: U32 = me.finished.peek()
        if me.live.peek() == 0
            break
        me.wait_on(at: me.finished, expected: seen)
        continue
    mask_interrupts()
    cpu_timer_stop()
    gic.turn_off(CPU_TIMER_LINE)
    gic.when_line_raised(line: CPU_TIMER_LINE, holder: bitcast(me.timer_before.peek()))
    RUNNING_ROUND_ROBIN.poke(.hollow())
    me.running.poke(.hollow())
    restore_interrupts(was)
    return

### Gives the core to the next ready thread, if there is one: the running thread goes to the end of the ready queue.
@target(arch: "aarch64", os: "none")
routine @ThreadManagerByRoundRobin.yield_now() -> None
    was: U64 = mask_interrupts()
    me.make_ready(me.running.peek())
    me.switch_away()
    restore_interrupts(was)
    return

### Puts the running thread to sleep for `span` at least: the other threads run meanwhile, and it is ready again once
### the counter has gone that far. A span of zero or less is `yield_now`.
### :param span: How long it sleeps.
@target(arch: "aarch64", os: "none")
routine @ThreadManagerByRoundRobin.sleep(span: Duration) -> None
    was: U64 = mask_interrupts()
    thread: @RoundRobinThread = me.running.peek()
    ticks: U64 = cpu_timer_ticks(span)
    if ticks == 0
        me.make_ready(thread)
        me.switch_away()
        restore_interrupts(was)
        return
    thread.wake_at.poke(cpu_timer_count() +^ ticks)
    thread.state.poke(RoundRobinState.SLEEPING)
    me.add_sleeper(thread)
    me.switch_away()
    restore_interrupts(was)
    return

### Puts the running thread to sleep while the U32 at `at` still holds `expected`, until `wake_one` or `wake_all` on
### `at`. It may also return without a wake, so the caller checks its condition again in a loop. A word that holds
### another value already returns at once.
### :param at: The word to wait on.
### :param expected: The value it must still hold for the thread to wait.
@target(arch: "aarch64", os: "none")
routine @ThreadManagerByRoundRobin.wait_on(at: @U32, expected: U32) -> None
    was: U64 = mask_interrupts()
    if at.peek_volatile() != expected
        restore_interrupts(was)
        return
    thread: @RoundRobinThread = me.running.peek()
    thread.waits_on.poke(at)
    thread.state.poke(RoundRobinState.WAITING)
    me.add_waiter(thread)
    me.switch_away()
    restore_interrupts(was)
    return

### Makes the thread that has waited longest on `at` ready, if any.
### :param at: The word the thread waits on.
@target(arch: "aarch64", os: "none")
routine @ThreadManagerByRoundRobin.wake_one(at: @U32) -> None
    was: U64 = mask_interrupts()
    me.wake_waiters(at, all: false)
    restore_interrupts(was)
    return

### Makes every thread waiting on `at` ready.
### :param at: The word the threads wait on.
@target(arch: "aarch64", os: "none")
routine @ThreadManagerByRoundRobin.wake_all(at: @U32) -> None
    was: U64 = mask_interrupts()
    me.wake_waiters(at, all: true)
    restore_interrupts(was)
    return

### The running thread's number: from 1 in the order threads were launched, 0 for the flow of `run`.
### :returns: The number.
@target(arch: "aarch64", os: "none")
routine @ThreadManagerByRoundRobin.current_number() -> U64
    return me.running.peek().number.peek()

### How many times the timer's interrupt has come while the core waited on the idle thread (`wfi`).
### :returns: The count.
@target(arch: "aarch64", os: "none")
routine @ThreadManagerByRoundRobin.idle_wakes() -> U64
    return me.idle_wakes.peek()

### How many times the timer has taken the core from a running thread and given it to another.
### :returns: The count.
@target(arch: "aarch64", os: "none")
routine @ThreadManagerByRoundRobin.preemptions() -> U64
    return me.preemptions.peek()

# -- the lists, with interrupts masked -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

### The bytes a slot keeps for its record, at the slot's start, rounded up to 16.
### :returns: The bytes.
@target(arch: "aarch64", os: "none")
secret routine record_bytes() -> U64
    room: U64 = sizeof[RoundRobinThread]()
    return (room + 15) - ((room + 15) & 15)

### The bytes a slot keeps below the stack: its record, its thread-local block on a 16-byte boundary (up to 16 more
### bytes when the slot starts off one), and 16 for the check word.
### :returns: The bytes.
@target(arch: "aarch64", os: "none")
secret routine record_room() -> U64
    return record_bytes() + thread_local_block_size() + 32

### Cuts the region into slots the first time it is asked: the idle thread in the first, the rest free.
@target(arch: "aarch64", os: "none")
secret routine @ThreadManagerByRoundRobin.prepare_slots() -> None
    if me.idle.peek().is_not_hollow()
        return
    base: U64 = ptrtoint[Addr, U64](me.region.peek())
    size: U64 = me.slot_size.peek()
    each i in 0 til me.slot_count.peek()
        at: U64 = base + i * size
        thread: @RoundRobinThread = inttoptr[U64, @RoundRobinThread](at)
        bottom: U64 = at + record_room()
        thread.poke(RoundRobinThread.construct(
            stack: StackManager.construct(bottom: inttoptr[U64, Addr](bottom), top: inttoptr[U64, Addr](at + size)),
            state: RoundRobinState.FREE))
        locals_at: U64 = at + record_bytes() + 15
        thread.thread_locals.poke(inttoptr[U64, Addr](locals_at - (locals_at & 15)))
        STACK_CHECK_WORD.poke_into(inttoptr[U64, @U64](bottom - sizeof[U64]()))
        if i > 0
            thread.next.poke(me.free.peek())
            me.free.poke(thread)
    idle: @RoundRobinThread = inttoptr[U64, @RoundRobinThread](base)
    idle.state.poke(RoundRobinState.READY)
    idle.stack.sp.poke(initial_stack(stack: idle.stack.bottom.peek(),
        size: idle.stack.size().bytes(), entry: round_robin_idle_main.to[Callable]().addr()))
    me.idle.poke(idle)
    return

### Puts `thread` at the end of the ready queue. The idle thread never goes there: it runs when the queue is empty.
### :param thread: The thread.
@target(arch: "aarch64", os: "none")
secret routine @ThreadManagerByRoundRobin.make_ready(thread: @RoundRobinThread) -> None
    if thread === me.idle.peek()
        return
    thread.state.poke(RoundRobinState.READY)
    thread.next.poke(.hollow())
    last: @RoundRobinThread = me.ready_last.peek()
    if last.is_hollow()
        me.ready_first.poke(thread)
    else
        last.next.poke(thread)
    me.ready_last.poke(thread)
    return

### Takes the first thread of the ready queue, or the idle thread when the queue is empty.
### :returns: The thread.
@target(arch: "aarch64", os: "none")
secret routine @ThreadManagerByRoundRobin.take_ready() -> @RoundRobinThread
    first: @RoundRobinThread = me.ready_first.peek()
    if first.is_hollow()
        return me.idle.peek()
    me.ready_first.poke(first.next.peek())
    if first.next.peek().is_hollow()
        me.ready_last.poke(.hollow())
    first.next.poke(.hollow())
    return first

### Puts a sleeping thread among the sleepers, after every one that wakes no later.
### :param thread: The thread, its `wake_at` set.
@target(arch: "aarch64", os: "none")
secret routine @ThreadManagerByRoundRobin.add_sleeper(thread: @RoundRobinThread) -> None
    wake_at: U64 = thread.wake_at.peek()
    first: @RoundRobinThread = me.sleeping.peek()
    if first.is_hollow() or wake_at < first.wake_at.peek()
        thread.next.poke(first)
        me.sleeping.poke(thread)
        return
    loop with after: @RoundRobinThread = first
        later: @RoundRobinThread = after.next.peek()
        if later.is_hollow() or wake_at < later.wake_at.peek()
            thread.next.poke(later)
            after.next.poke(thread)
            return
        continue with after: later

### Puts a waiting thread at the end of the waiters, so a wake finds the one that waited longest first.
### :param thread: The thread, its `waits_on` set.
@target(arch: "aarch64", os: "none")
secret routine @ThreadManagerByRoundRobin.add_waiter(thread: @RoundRobinThread) -> None
    thread.next.poke(.hollow())
    first: @RoundRobinThread = me.waiting.peek()
    if first.is_hollow()
        me.waiting.poke(thread)
        return
    loop with after: @RoundRobinThread = first
        later: @RoundRobinThread = after.next.peek()
        if later.is_hollow()
            after.next.poke(thread)
            return
        continue with after: later

### Makes every sleeper whose time has come ready, in the order they wake.
### :param now: The counter now.
@target(arch: "aarch64", os: "none")
secret routine @ThreadManagerByRoundRobin.wake_sleepers(now: U64) -> None
    loop
        first: @RoundRobinThread = me.sleeping.peek()
        if first.is_hollow() or first.wake_at.peek() > now
            return
        me.sleeping.poke(first.next.peek())
        me.make_ready(first)
        continue

### Makes the threads waiting on `at` ready: the one that waited longest, or all of them.
### :param at: The word.
### :param all: Whether every waiter on `at` wakes.
@target(arch: "aarch64", os: "none")
secret routine @ThreadManagerByRoundRobin.wake_waiters(at: @U32, all: Bool) -> None
    place: Addr = at
    loop with before: @RoundRobinThread = .hollow(), thread: @RoundRobinThread = me.waiting.peek()
        if thread.is_hollow()
            return
        later: @RoundRobinThread = thread.next.peek()
        if thread.waits_on.peek() !== place
            continue with before: thread, thread: later
        if before.is_hollow()
            me.waiting.poke(later)
        else
            before.next.poke(later)
        thread.waits_on.poke(.hollow())
        me.make_ready(thread)
        if not all
            return
        continue with before: before, thread: later

### Sets the timer for the next thing the manager must do: the end of the running thread's slice when another thread
### is ready, else the first sleeper's time, else nothing (the timer stops).
@target(arch: "aarch64", os: "none")
secret routine @ThreadManagerByRoundRobin.arm_timer() -> None
    now: U64 = cpu_timer_count()
    sleeper: @RoundRobinThread = me.sleeping.peek()
    if me.ready_first.peek().is_not_hollow()
        deadline: U64 = now +^ me.slice.peek()
        if sleeper.is_not_hollow()
            cpu_timer_raise_at(deadline.min(sleeper.wake_at.peek()))
            return
        cpu_timer_raise_at(deadline)
        return
    if sleeper.is_not_hollow()
        cpu_timer_raise_at(sleeper.wake_at.peek())
        return
    cpu_timer_stop()
    return

### Switches from the running thread, which is in a list already (or done), to the next ready one, after setting the
### timer for it. Called with interrupts masked, it returns when something switches back to this thread.
@target(arch: "aarch64", os: "none")
secret routine @ThreadManagerByRoundRobin.switch_away() -> None
    from: @RoundRobinThread = me.running.peek()
    to: @RoundRobinThread = me.take_ready()
    if to === from
        from.state.poke(RoundRobinState.RUNNING)
        me.arm_timer()
        return
    me.check_stack(from)
    to.state.poke(RoundRobinState.RUNNING)
    me.running.poke(to)
    me.arm_timer()
    trace_switch(to.trace_depth.peek()).poke_into(from.trace_depth)
    # The frame check measures from the bottom of the stack it runs on: the thread's slot, or the program's own.
    if to === me.boot
        RUNNING_STACK.poke(.hollow())
    else
        RUNNING_STACK.poke(to.stack)
    set_thread_pointer(to.thread_locals.peek())
    resume_at: Addr = to.stack.sp.peek()
    to.stack.sp.poke(.hollow())
    switch_fiber(from: from.stack.sp, to: resume_at)
    return

### Stops the program with `StackOverflowError` when `thread`'s stack ran into its check word. The flow of `run` is on
### the program's own stack, which StackManager checks.
### :param thread: The thread that stops.
@target(arch: "aarch64", os: "none")
secret routine @ThreadManagerByRoundRobin.check_stack(thread: @RoundRobinThread) -> None
    if thread === me.boot
        return
    check: @U64 = inttoptr[U64, @U64](ptrtoint[Addr, U64](thread.stack.bottom.peek()) - sizeof[U64]())
    if check.peek() != STACK_CHECK_WORD
        throw StackOverflowError(description: "A thread's stack ran out: its routines called each other deeper than its slot holds.")
    return

# -- the threads' first routines and the timer -----------------------------------------------------------------------

### A thread's first routine: it reserves the thread's failure mold, lets interrupts in, runs the thread's routine, and
### once that returns, gives the slot back and switches away for good.
@target(arch: "aarch64", os: "none")
@noreturn
secret routine round_robin_thread_main() -> None
    manager: @ThreadManagerByRoundRobin = RUNNING_ROUND_ROBIN.peek()
    thread: @RoundRobinThread = manager.running.peek()
    RUNNING_MOLD.poke(reserve_failure_mold())
    ThreadLocalManager.reset()
    unmask_interrupts()
    entry: Callable[(Addr,), None] = thread.entry.peek()
    entry.call(thread.arg.peek())
    mask_interrupts()
    manager.check_stack(thread)
    thread.state.poke(RoundRobinState.FREE)
    thread.next.poke(manager.free.peek())
    manager.free.poke(thread)
    manager.live.peek().sub(1).poke_into(manager.live)
    manager.finished.peek().add(1).poke_into(manager.finished)
    manager.wake_waiters(at: manager.finished, all: true)
    # The slot is free from here, and nothing launches into it before this switch: interrupts stay masked.
    loop
        manager.switch_away()

### The idle thread: it waits for an interrupt with interrupts let in, so the timer's handler can give the core to a
### thread that became ready. Another interrupt's handler may make a thread ready too, which it switches to itself.
@target(arch: "aarch64", os: "none")
@noreturn
secret routine round_robin_idle_main() -> None
    manager: @ThreadManagerByRoundRobin = RUNNING_ROUND_ROBIN.peek()
    RUNNING_MOLD.poke(reserve_failure_mold())
    loop
        unmask_interrupts()
        wait_for_next_interrupt()
        mask_interrupts()
        if manager.ready_first.peek().is_not_hollow()
            manager.switch_away()
        continue

### The timer's interrupt: the sleepers whose time has come are made ready, and the running thread, when another one is
### ready, goes to the end of the ready queue for it. Runs on the interrupted thread's stack, with interrupts masked.
### :param line: The timer's line.
@target(arch: "aarch64", os: "none")
secret routine round_robin_tick(line: U32) -> None
    manager: @ThreadManagerByRoundRobin = RUNNING_ROUND_ROBIN.peek()
    if manager.is_hollow()
        cpu_timer_stop()
        return
    manager.wake_sleepers(cpu_timer_count())
    running: @RoundRobinThread = manager.running.peek()
    if running === manager.idle.peek()
        manager.idle_wakes.peek().add(1).poke_into(manager.idle_wakes)
    if manager.ready_first.peek().is_hollow()
        manager.arm_timer()
        return
    if running !== manager.idle.peek()
        manager.preemptions.peek().add(1).poke_into(manager.preemptions)
    manager.make_ready(running)
    manager.switch_away()
    return
